A blind SQL injection payload designed to reveal server version information could give an attacker visibility into vulnerable systems. The pattern works by injecting malicious SQL into vulnerable endpoints, then extracting metadata from the database response to determine which version of SQL Server the application was running on.

What you'd typically see in a legitimate test environment:

```sql
SELECT @vendor = 'Microsoft';
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
INSERT INTO customers VALUES(1, '{"name"':"'jose'','"id":...}');
```

If you reverse-engineered that script line-by-line, you'd find:

```sql
-- example.com
SELECT @vendor = 'Microsoft';
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
INSERT INTO customers VALUES(1, '{"name"':"'jose'','"id":...}');
```

The ``id`` parameter will return the version number in the response.

**Why this matters:** Blind SQL injection is CVE-2008-4656, so organizations patch against it immediately. If you're building defenses, look at SQL Server's built-in version metadata (SHOW VERSION), and consider implementing parameterized queries where possible.

